###### Abstract

The Be/X-ray binary A 0535+26 showed a normal (type I) outburst in August 2009. It is the fourth in a series of normal outbursts associated with the periastron, but is unusual by presenting a double-peaked light curve. The two peaks reached a flux of \\sim 450  mCrab in the 15-50 keV range. We present results of the timing and spectral analysis of INTEGRAL, RXTE, and Suzaku observations of the outburst. The energy dependent pulse profiles and their evolution during the outburst are studied. No significant differences with respect to other normal outbursts are observed. The centroid energy of the fundamental cyclotron line shows no significant variation during the outburst. A spectral hardening with increasing luminosity is observed. We conclude that the source is accreting in the sub-critical regime. We discuss possible explanations for the double-peaked outburst.

Subject headings:pulsars: individual (A 0535+26) -- stars: magnetic field-- X-rays: binaries -- X-rays: stars

## 1. Introduction

The Be/X-ray binary A 0535+26 was discovered during a giant outburst with Ariel V (Rosenberg et al., 1975). At a distance of d\\sim 2 kpc, it consists of the B0 IIlle optical companion HDE 245770 (Steele et al., 1998) and a pulsating neutron star of spin period P_{\\rm spin}\\sim 103 s, in an eccentric orbit (e=0.47) of period of P_{\\rm orb}=111.1 days (Finger et al., 2006). The system presents different luminosity states associated with the activity of the Be star: quiescence, with X-ray luminosities L_{\\rm X}\\lesssim 10^{36} erg s{}^{-1}(Rothschild et al., 2012), normal (type I) outbursts, with luminosities L_{\\rm X}\\sim 10^{36-37} erg s{}^{-1}, and giant (type II) outbursts, that can reach luminosities L_{\\rm X}\\gtrsim 10^{37} erg s{}^{-1}(see, e.g., Finger, Wilson & Harmon, 1996). A 0535+26 presents two cyclotron resonance scattering features (CRSFs or cyclotron lines) in its X-ray spectrum at \\sim46 and \\sim100 keV (see, e.g., Caballero et al., 2007, and references therein). Cyclotron lines are caused by resonant scattering of photons off electrons in the quantized Landau levels, and the cyclotron line energy is proportional to the magnetic field strength, E_{\\rm cyc}=\\frac{\\hbar B}{B}/m_{\\rm e}c=11.6 keV \\cdotB/10^{12} G (e.g., Schonherr et al., 2007).

Some accreting X-ray pulsars present a negative correlation between the cyclotron line centroid energy and the X-ray luminosity (e.g., 4U 0115+6415, V 0332+53, see Nakajima et al., 2006; Tsygankov et al., 2006; Morbavil et al., 2006). Other sources, like Her X-1, GX 304-1, and Swift J1626.6-5156 show the opposite trend (Staubert et al., 2007; Klochkov et al., 2012, DeCesar et al., 2012). As discussed by Becker et al. (2012), this bimodality is probably due to two different accretion regimes depending on the critical luminosity L_{\\rm crit}. For sources with L_{\\rm X}>L_{\\rm crit} (_supercritical_ sources), a radiative shock decelerates the infalling matter, with the height of the emission region increasing with increasing mass accretion rate, explaining the negative correlation between the cyclotron line energy and the X-ray luminosity. On the other hand, for sources with L_{\\rm X}<L_{\\rm crit} (_subcritical_ sources), Coulomb interactions stop the infalling matter, and the height of the emission region decreases with increasing accretion rate, explaining the positive correlation observed (further details in Becker et al., 2012, see also Basko & Sunyaev, 1976, Staubert et al., 2007). Contrary to these sources, for A 0535+26 no significant correlation between the cyclotron line energy and the X-ray luminosity has been observed (Terada et al., 2006; Caballero et al., 2007), with the exception of a flaring episode during the onset of a normal outburst observed in 2005 during which the cyclotron line energy significantly increased (see Caballero et al., 2008; Postnov et al., 2008 for details). These results refer to pulse phase averaged spectroscopy; see however Klochkov et al. (2011) for pulse-to-pulse analysis in which a positive correlation between the flux and the cyclotron line energy was observed.

Footnote 15: See, however, Müller et al. (2012b), where it is shown that for 4U 0115+64 the presence of a correlation depends on the model for the continuum.

A 0535+26 showed a giant outburst and a series of normal ones in 2009, all of them associated with the periastron passage of the neutron star (Caballero et al., 2009). The giant outburst reached a flux of F_{(15-50)\\,\\rm keV}=(1.17\\pm 0.04)\\,\\rm counts\\,s^{-1}\\,cm^{-2} (\\sim 5.3\\,\\rm Crab16) at MJD 55179.0 and lasted about 40 days. In this work we focus on a double-peaked outburst that preceded the giant one. We present _INTEGRAL_, RXTE, and Suzaku observations of the outburst. The Swift/BAT light curve of the outburst and the times of the observations are shown in Fig. 1 (a). The first peak of the outburst reached a flux of F_{(15-50)\\,\\rm keV}=(0.100\\pm 0.004)\\,\\rm counts\\,s^{-1}\\,cm^{-2} (\\sim 455\\,\\rm mCrab16) at MJD 55048.0, that decreased to F_{(15-50)\\,\\rm keV}=(0.047\\pm 0.002)\\,\\rm counts\\,s^{-1}\\,cm^{-2} (\\sim214\\,\\rm Crab) at MJD 55051.0 and rose again, reaching F_{(15-50)\\,\\rm keV}=(0.104\\pm 0.005)\\,\\rm counts\\,s^{-1}\\,cm^{-2} (\\sim471\\,\\rm mCrab) at MJD 55058.0 around periastron. The observations are described in Sec. 2. The results of the timing and spectral analyses are presented in Sec. 3, and the results are discussed in Sec. 4.

